      "math_academy_id": "q-161682",
      "sequence_position": 12,
      "problem": "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the surfaces $x=-{z}^{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{y},$ and the planes $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ $z=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,-1\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,-{z}^{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[x]_{x=-{z}^{2}}^{x=\\sqrt{y}}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{y}-(-{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[z\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{z=-1}^{z=2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-(-1))\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}-(-1)^{3})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\sqrt{y}+3\\,dy \\\\  & ={[2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}+3y]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}+3(1)-0 \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$",
